\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Computational evaluation mechanism. A fixed contact-derived graph supplies the full and degree-restricted searches. Each search is repeated at nested selection-world budgets under common tie-breaking. Ordered traces are compared across budgets and candidate pools, while selected sets are evaluated on disjoint worlds. The network motifs, world tiles, and seed tokens are schematic, not measured data; tile counts are not proportional to simulation budgets. Muted nodes remain in the propagation graph but are ineligible as seeds. Arrows indicate computational information flow, not an experimentally established causal mechanism for trace instability. An editable native Visio master accompanies the figure.}}{4}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:1}{{1}{4}{Computational evaluation mechanism. A fixed contact-derived graph supplies the full and degree-restricted searches. Each search is repeated at nested selection-world budgets under common tie-breaking. Ordered traces are compared across budgets and candidate pools, while selected sets are evaluated on disjoint worlds. The network motifs, world tiles, and seed tokens are schematic, not measured data; tile counts are not proportional to simulation budgets. Muted nodes remain in the propagation graph but are ineligible as seeds. Arrows indicate computational information flow, not an experimentally established causal mechanism for trace instability. An editable native Visio master accompanies the figure}{figure.1}{}}
